#!/usr/bin/env bash

lordhavemercy.old -f --verbose << "EOF"

Add a script called bin/redact that supports:

(1) the R algorithm, and (2) the RJE algorithm.

That is, the redact command will take in text from stdin or argv filenames, like grep, and generate text to stdout (or by making changes to the file in question if run with -i/--in-place, like sed.)

The output text merges whatever stdin + argv input files and prints to stdout a single stream of text, Diatesseron or Torah style, the way that the R source would have combined JE and P, or in the way that the RJE source would have combined J and E, depending on the values of several parameters, described below.

Note: in both algorithms, the redactor (you) *adds* very little text, mostly just glue prose.

But RJE is allowed to delete redundant things that appear in both sources, while R keeps both sources intact and is willing to have doublets.

The algorithm is supposed to produce the best version of the input content by adding as little as possible to fill logical gaps, typos, thoughts being in the wrong place, etc. But the user gets to control how that happens.

The algorithm is allowed to:
 - transition from one source to the other mid sentence or between sentences, whenever is best.
 - add small amounts of glue prose
 - delete small or large amounts of redundant stuff within or between sources
 - reshuffle existing sentences or paragraphs (though how much that's allowed depends on a parameter below)
 - it is constrained by the fact that it is not allowed to generate *large* amounts
   of text that weren't already in the original source or sources, and it's
   generally encouraged not to reshuffle or reorder lines or sentences or paragraphs
   within a given source unless a parameter is set as described below.

Here's the command:

redact [-d/--del n] [-a/--add n] [-s/--src [file|paragraph|sentence|word]] [--rje|--rip] [-i/--inplace] <file-or-files-or-stdin>

The --del <n> and --add <n> options should behave like gzip, to control the level of RJE / R ness, namely

    gzip has:

     -# --fast --best
            Regulate the speed of compression using the specified digit #,
            where -1 or --fast indicates the fastest compression method (less compression)
            and  -9  or  --best indicates the slowest compression method (best compression).
            The default compression level is -6 (that is, biased towards high compression at expense of speed).

By default, the program should work in R mode.

R mode (redact / redact --rip / the default) is an abbreviation for running with the parameters
    --add 4
    --del 2
    because R (Ezra) is willing to add a bit more than RJE, but barely ever deletes, leading to doublets.

RJE mode (redact --rje) is an abbreviation for running with the parameters
    --add 2
    --del 4
    because RJE was willing to del more than R, but barely ever adds, leading to missing bits of the original J and E sources.

bin/redact should support all this using a software 1.0 shell script that parses all the options and then uses them to run
a law/lawd/lord/lordy/lordhavemercy heredoc at the bottom of the script that explains everything I've just explained to you.

bin/redact2 should attempt to accomplish the same things as redact but ONLY by using the heredoc.

That is:
    - redact parses options in the usual way and calls lord, lordhavemercy, or whatever with a prompt describing the options the user passed *on this run* and what they mean.
    - redact2 does the entire thing with one lordhavemercy heredoc with unquoted `<< EOF` so that variables expand within the heredoc, and redact2 simply describes the command how I've just described it to you and says to act like that.

Finally, make sure if I pass in -f/--force to redact, that flag gets passed to the law/lawd/lord/lordy/lordhavemercy commands.
However, if I pass -f/--force to redact2, don't pass it to the top level lordhavemercy heredoc runner, just SAY that -f/--force was passed in, or rather don't say it. Let the agent know by giving it the $@ inside its heredoc the same way you pass everything else.

Do that now.
EOF
